<chapter
 id="lecture10">
#title("Lazy Stream-Processing")

#para("\

It could simply be put that the real fun part of functional
programming starts with streams.

")

#para("\

#emphasis("A note on the dialect.") The lazy streams of the GC era (the type
#stacode("stream"), the construct #keycode("$delay") and the function
#dyncode("lazy_force"), which cached the evaluated cells: memoization) do
not exist any more: a suspended computation or a cell of a stream is allocated on
heap, and nothing but a garbage collector could reclaim it when it is shared. What
this chapter and the next one present are the lazy streams that remain: the
#emphasis("linear") streams, of the type #stacode("stream_vt"), which are made
with the construct #keycode("$ldelay"). A linear stream is used only once (forcing
it consumes it), there is no caching, and a stream that is not needed any
more has to be freed. The functions below show how functional stream-processing is done under these
rules.

")

#para("\

A stream is like a list but it is lazy in the sense that the elements
in a stream are not required to be made available at the moment when
the stream is formed. Instead, each element only needs to be produced
at the point where the element is actually needed for evaluation.
Given a type T, the type #stacode("stream_vt(T)") is for a value
representing a stream of elements of the type T. Let us use the name
stream-value to refer to such a value, which is completely opaque for
it does not even reveal whether the stream it represents is empty or
not. Internally, a stream-value is represented as a thunk, that is, a
nullary closure-function (it lives on heap and it is linear). Evaluating a stream-value
(that is, calling the thunk representing it, which is written with the
prefix operator #keycode("!")) yields a stream-con value of type
#dyncode("stream_vt_con(T)") for some T, where #dyncode("stream_vt_con")
is the following declared linear datatype (in the prelude):

#sats2xhtml('\
//
datavtype
stream_vt_con
  (a:vt@ype+) =
  | stream_vt_nil of ((*void*))
  | stream_vt_cons of (a, stream_vt(a))
//
where
stream_vt
  (a:vt@ype) = lazy_vt(stream_vt_con(a))
//
')

With pattern matching, we can inspect whether a stream-con value
represents an empty stream or not. If the stream is non-empty, then
its first element can be extracted. The type #stacode("lazy_vt(T)") is
essentially for a thunk that returns a value of the type T. Evaluating a
stream-value consumes the thunk (and the memory of the thunk is reclaimed), and the
stream-con value that it yields is to be consumed, too: in a pattern, the symbol
#keycode("~") says that the cell is freed and that the head and the tail of
the stream are now owned by the program.

")

#para("\

As an example, the following function #dyncode("int_stream_from")
takes an integer #dyncode("n") and returns a stream that enumerates
ascendingly all of the integers greater than or equal to
#dyncode("n"):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
int_stream_from(n: int): stream_vt(int)
//
implement
int_stream_from(n) =
$ldelay(stream_vt_cons(n, int_stream_from(n+1)))
//
')

The keyword #keycode("$ldelay") indicates the construction
of a thunk based on the expression appearing as its argument. Calling
#dyncode("int_stream_from") returns immediately as all that is done is
creating a thunk.

")

#para("\

Accessing elements in a given stream is shown in the following
example:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
stream_get_at
(xs: stream_vt(a), n: int): a
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
stream_get_at(xs, n) =
(
case+ !xs of
| ~stream_vt_nil() =>
  (
    $raise StreamSubscriptExn()
  )
| ~stream_vt_cons(x, xs) =>
  (
    if n <= 0 then (~xs; x) else stream_get_at<a>(xs, n-1)
  )
)
//
')

The function #dyncode("stream_get_at") is the stream-version of
#dyncode("list_get_at"): Given a stream and a position (denoted by an
integer), #dyncode("stream_get_at") returns the element in the stream
at the given position. Note that the symbol #dyncode("!") refers to
the function #dyncode("lazy_vt_force"), which evaluates a given stream
value into a stream-con value in this case. The stream is consumed: the cells
before the element are freed as they are passed, and the rest of the stream (which was
never evaluated) is freed by #dyncode("~xs"), the abbreviation of
#dyncode("lazy_vt_free(xs)"). Like
#dyncode("list_get_at"), it is almost always a poor style of
programming to make use of #dyncode("stream_get_at") excessively (for
instance, inside the body of a loop).

")

#para("\

Let us see what is involved in the evaluation of the following code:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
// A stream can be used only once: its first million elements are
// consumed (and freed one by one) by [stream_get_at]
//
val xs = int_stream_from(0)
val x0 = stream_get_at<int>(xs, 1000000)
val () = println! ("x0 = ", x0)
//
')

The call to #dyncode("int_stream_from") returns immediately as all
that is essentially done is creating a thunk (that is, a nullary
closure-function). The call to #dyncode("stream_get_at") returns
1000000 after creating 1000001 nodes one after another, and each node
is freed as soon as the next element is requested. Therefore the whole
evaluation takes a tiny amount of memory. In the GC era, a call to
#dyncode("lazy_force") evaluated its argument to a value and cached the value, so that the 1000001
nodes created by a call to #dyncode("stream_get_at") were kept in memory as long
as the stream was in use: this is the memoization, which is expensive
memory-wise and unpredicatable time-wise. A linear stream involves
no memoization, and the internal representation of a stream only needs memory for
storing one node (that contains the head element of the stream).

")

#para("\

Basically, for each a list-processing function, there is a
corresponding version of stream-processing function. Let us see some
concrete examples.  For instance, the following function
#dyncode("stream_append") (corresponding to #dyncode("list_append"))
concatenates two given streams, both of which are consumed:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
stream_append
(xs: stream_vt(a), ys: stream_vt(a)): stream_vt(a)
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
stream_append
(xs, ys) = $ldelay
(
(
case+ !xs of
| ~stream_vt_nil() => !ys
| ~stream_vt_cons(x, xs) =>
  stream_vt_cons(x, stream_append<a>(xs, ys))
) : stream_vt_con(a)
,
(~xs; ~ys) // called when the stream is freed
)
//
')

Please note the symbol #dyncode("!") in front of #dyncode("!ys"):
The expression following #keycode("$ldelay") should evaluate to
a stream-con value (instead of a stream value). The expression following
the comma is evaluated when the stream is freed before it is forced:
it frees the two streams that the thunk holds. A thunk is created with two
pieces of code: the first is for evaluating it and the second for freeing
it, and a thunk with no second piece of code frees nothing (see
the next chapter).

")

#para("\

Improper use of streams is abundant in practice.  In particular, it is
very common to see streams being treated as lists, which defeats the
purpose of having streams in the first place. I would like to make a
point on proper stream construction by comparing three implementations
of the following function #dyncode("stream_make_list") for turning a
given list (which is consumed) into a stream:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
stream_make_list(xs: List0_vt(a)): stream_vt(a)
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
stream_make_list
  (xs) = $ldelay
(
(
case+ xs of
| ~list_vt_nil() =>
  stream_vt_nil()
| ~list_vt_cons(x, xs) =>
  stream_vt_cons(x, stream_make_list<a>(xs))
) : stream_vt_con(a)
,
list_vt_free<a>(xs)
) (* end of [stream_make_list] *)
//
')

This one is what I consider to be a proper implementation
of stream construction. It is #emphasis("fully lazy") in the
sense that the implementation does nothing when called
upon a given list-value except for constructing a thunk
(that represents the returned stream); the list is held by the thunk, and it is
freed by the second piece of code if the stream is freed before the thunk is
evaluated.

The next implementation of #dyncode("stream_make_list") is a slight
variant of the previous one:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
// The second implementation of [stream_make_list]: it is not fully lazy
// as it inspects the list before it constructs the thunk
//
fun
{a:t@ype}
stream_make_list2
  (xs: List0_vt(a)): stream_vt(a) =
(
case+ xs of
| ~list_vt_nil() =>
  $ldelay(stream_vt_nil())
| ~list_vt_cons(x, xs) =>
  $ldelay
  ( stream_vt_cons(x, stream_make_list2<a>(xs))
  , list_vt_free<a>(xs) // called when the stream is freed
  )
) (* end of [stream_make_list2] *)
//
// The third implementation of [stream_make_list]: the stream is
// not lazy at all (the whole list is turned into nodes of the
// stream before the function returns)
//
fun
{a:t@ype}
stream_make_list3
  (xs: List0_vt(a)): stream_vt(a) =
(
case+ xs of
| ~list_vt_nil() =>
  $ldelay(stream_vt_nil())
| ~list_vt_cons(x, xs) => let
    val xs = stream_make_list3<a>(xs)
  in
    $ldelay(stream_vt_cons(x, xs), ~xs)
  end // end of [list_vt_cons]
) (* end of [stream_make_list3] *)
//
val zs2 = stream2list_vt<int>(stream_make_list2<int>($list_vt{int}(1, 2, 3)))
val zs3 = stream2list_vt<int>(stream_make_list3<int>($list_vt{int}(1, 2, 3)))
val () = println! ("make_list2: ", zs2)
val () = println! ("make_list3: ", zs3)
val () = list_vt_free<int>(zs2)
val () = list_vt_free<int>(zs3)
//
')

Note the second implementation is not fully lazy as it inspects whether
the given list is empty before it constructs a thunk (that
represents the returned stream).

The third implementation of
#dyncode("stream_make_list") should definitely be
avoided: there is essentially no laziness involved in
the construction of the stream being generated, as the recursive call is made
before the thunk is created (and the whole list is turned into nodes at once).
This is a typical example that shows clearly what is meant by treating
a stream as a list.

")

#para("\

With streams, we can facilitate the use of (general) recursion in
problem-solving by eliminating the risk of stack overflow caused by
deeply nested recursive calls. In the case of
#dyncode("stream_append"), the evaluation of a call to
#dyncode("stream_append") returns immediately as all it does
essentially is to form a thunk (for representing the resulting
stream). On the other hand, the implementation of
#dyncode("list_append") presented previously can potentially cause
stack overflow if its argument is a long list (e.g., one consisting of
1000000 elements). By the way, the actual implementation of
#dyncode("list_vt_append") in #filename("ATSLIB/prelude") (the prelude
library of ATS) cannot cause stack overflow due to its being
tail-recursive. However, there is no free lunch here as this library
implementation is written in an advanced style that is somewhat
difficult to adopt in practice. A test of the two functions above:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
val xs123 = stream_make_list<int>($list_vt{int}(1, 2, 3))
val ys456 = stream_make_list<int>($list_vt{int}(4, 5, 6))
val xs123456 = stream_append<int>(xs123, ys456)
val zs = stream2list_vt<int>(xs123456)
val () = println! ("append: ", zs)
val () = list_vt_free<int>(zs)
//
')

")

#para("\

A function argument that is stored in a stream is a closure that has to
live as long as the stream does. It is a linear closure (the arrow
#stacode("-&lt;cloptr1&gt;")) that the stream owns: it is freed with the stream, and
it is freed when the end of the stream is reached. The function
#dyncode("cfun_free") frees a closure (it plays the role of the call to
#dyncode("cloptr_free"), which is a little verbose), and the following function
#dyncode("stream_map") is the stream-version of
#dyncode("list_map"):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
// a linear closure is freed by this function
//
fun
{a,b:t@ype}
cfun_free
(f: (a) -<cloptr1> b): void =
cloptr_free($UNSAFE.castvwtp0{cloptr(void)}(f))
//
')

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
{b:t@ype}
stream_map
(xs: stream_vt(a), fopr: (a) -<cloptr1> b): stream_vt(b)
//
implement
{a}{b}
stream_map
  (xs, fopr) = $ldelay
(
(
case+ !xs of
| ~stream_vt_nil() =>
  (cfun_free<a,b>(fopr); stream_vt_nil())
| ~stream_vt_cons(x, xs) =>
  stream_vt_cons(fopr(x), stream_map<a><b>(xs, fopr))
) : stream_vt_con(b)
,
(~xs; cfun_free<a,b>(fopr))
)
//
')

")

#para("\

The following function #dyncode("stream_filter") is the stream-version of
#dyncode("list_filter"):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
stream_filter
(xs: stream_vt(a), test: (a) -<cloptr1> bool): stream_vt(a)
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
stream_filter
  (xs, test) = $ldelay
(
(
case+ !xs of
| ~stream_vt_nil() =>
  (cfun_free<a,bool>(test); stream_vt_nil())
| ~stream_vt_cons(x, xs) =>
  if test(x)
    then
    stream_vt_cons
      (x, stream_filter<a>(xs, test))
    // end of [then]
    else !(stream_filter<a>(xs, test))
  // end of [if]
) : stream_vt_con(a)
,
(~xs; cfun_free<a,bool>(test))
)
//
')

")

#para("\

A function like #dyncode("stream_map") and #dyncode("stream_filter")
is often referred to as being fully lazy as its evaluation does nothing
except for creating a thunk (to represent a suspended computation).
They can be put to work together, as in the following code that finds
the 11th even square:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
val xs = int_stream_from(1)
val ys = stream_map<int><int>(xs, lam(x) =<cloptr1> x * x)
val zs = stream_filter<int>(ys, lam(x) =<cloptr1> x % 2 = 0)
val z10 = stream_get_at<int>(zs, 10)
val () = println! ("the 11th even square: ", z10)
//
')

")

#para("\

The following function #dyncode("sieve") implements the sieve of
Eratosthenes for enumerating prime numbers:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
sieve(): stream_vt(int)
//
implement
sieve() = let
//
fun
auxmain
(
xs: stream_vt(int)
) : stream_vt(int) = $ldelay
(
(
case+ !xs of
| ~stream_vt_nil() => stream_vt_nil()
| ~stream_vt_cons(x0, xs) =>
  stream_vt_cons
  ( x0
  , auxmain(stream_filter<int>(xs, lam(x) =<cloptr1> x % x0 > 0))
  ) // end of [stream_vt_cons]
) : stream_vt_con(int)
,
~xs
)
//
in
  auxmain(int_stream_from(2))
end // end of [sieve]
//
')

A call to #dyncode("sieve") returns a stream consisting of all the
primes enumerated in the ascending order: 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, etc. Note
that the closure passed to #dyncode("stream_filter") captures the integer
#dyncode("x0") (a value) and it is owned by the filtered stream; and that
the pattern #dyncode("~stream_vt_nil()") has to be covered, since the cell
is to be freed (the case is not possible for this stream).

")

#para("\

As a simple experiment, please try to evaluate the following code:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
val thePrimes = sieve()
val () = println! ("stream_get_at(thePrimes, 5000):")
val () = println! (stream_get_at<int>(thePrimes, 5000))
//
')

Note that the number #emphasis("48619") is to be printed out. There is a clear
pause before it is done. In the GC era, the same call was made twice and the
second call was done immediately thanks to memoization: here a stream can be used
only once, and a second call would need a second stream (that is, another run
of the same computation). A stream that is not consumed is freed by
#dyncode("~"):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
// A stream that is not consumed is freed by [~] (or by [lazy_vt_free])
//
val xs = int_stream_from(0)
val () = ~xs
//
')

")

#para("\

Lastly, let us see a simple but telling example that demonstrates a
stream-based approach to addressing the potential risk of stack
overflow due to deeply nested non-tail-recursive calls. The standard
implementation of #dyncode("list_map") (see the chapter on list-processing) is
not tail-recursive. When applied to a
long list (e.g., one consisting of 1000000 elements),
#dyncode("list_map") runs a high risk of causing stack overflow (I tried: the
program crashes). This can become a very serious issue in practice if we ever want to apply
functional programming to a domain like machine learning where
processing large data is a norm rather than an exception. One
possibility is to insist on using only tail-recursion in the
implementation of a function like #dyncode("list_map") that may need
to be applied to large data, but such a requirement or restriction can
clearly exert negative impact on the use of recursion in
problem-solving. After all, there are numerous algorithms that are
natrually expressed in terms of (general) recursion. And non-trivial
effort is often needed in order to implement such an algorithm based
on tail-recursion only, likely diminishing programming productivity.

")

#para("\

Another implementation of a mapping function on lists is given as follows
(the list is consumed by the function, as it is held by the thunks of the stream,
and the function is an envless one):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
// The standard implementation of [list_map] is not tail-recursive
//
fun
{a,b:t@ype}
list_map_stream
(xs: List0_vt(a), fopr: (a) -> b): List0_vt(b) = let
//
fun
auxmain
(
xs: List0_vt(a), fopr: (a) -> b
) : stream_vt(b) = $ldelay
(
(
case+ xs of
| ~list_vt_nil() => stream_vt_nil()
| ~list_vt_cons(x, xs) => stream_vt_cons(fopr(x), auxmain(xs, fopr))
) : stream_vt_con(b)
,
list_vt_free<a>(xs)
)
//
in
  stream2list_vt<b>(auxmain(xs, fopr))
end // end of [list_map_stream]
//
val xs = list_make_intrange(0, 1000000)
val ys = list_map_stream<int,int>(xs, lam(x) => x + 1)
val () = println! ("length(ys) = ", list_vt_length<int>(ys))
val () = list_vt_free<int>(ys)
//
')

What is special about this implementation
lies in the implementation of the inner function #dyncode("auxmain"),
which turns a list into a stream. Calling #dyncode("auxmain") runs no
risk of stack overflow as it simply creates a thunk (without issuing
any recursive calls).  The library function #dyncode("stream2list_vt")
turns a stream into a (linear) list. As
#dyncode("stream2list_vt") is implemented tail-recursively, it can be
safely called to generate a long list.  Consequently, this
stream-based implementation of the mapping function can be applied to
a long list with no concern of causing stack overflow (the code applies it to a list of
1000000 elements). After linear
lazy streams are looked at more closely, we are to see a
version of this stream-based approach that does not even need a list.

")

#para("\

Please find #mycodelink("LECTURE/10/CODE", "on-line") the entirety of
the code used in this chapter. It was compiled with #command("patscc")
(no flag is needed) and run with the address sanitizer, which reports no
leak. \

")#comment("para")

</chapter><!--id="lecture10"-->

